    {
      "id": "30000101",
      "type": "article-journal",
      "title": "Streetlight-Moth Interactions in Meadows Bordering Rural Roads Lit Since the 1970s.",
      "container-title": "Journal of nocturnal ecology",
      "container-title-short": "J Noct Ecol",
      "volume": "373",
      "issue": "3",
      "page": "220-31",
      "abstract": "BACKGROUND: Moths are among the most important pollinators of meadow plants at night, and artificial light draws them away from flowers. METHODS: We conducted two paired, replicated, observer-blind, field surveys (LANTERN and BEACON) that were designed to assess the effects of streetlights in combination with road traffic on moths in meadows 12 metres or closer to a road that had been lit since the 1970s. CONCLUSIONS: These data show that streetlights in combination with traffic reduced the visits of moths to flowers in meadows bordering the road. (Funded by the Brisbane Field Naturalists and others; LANTERN and BEACON survey numbers, S-01807923 and S-01807949.)",
      "language": "eng",
